WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
First principles thinking is the closest

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:08.160
thing to a cheat code for life.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.760
Successful people from Elon Musk all the

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.120
way to Kobe Bryant used it to break

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.520
conventions and become one of a kind.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.880
First principles make you stand apart. I

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.320
got to use it, too, from MIT all the way

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.400
to billion-dollar boardrooms. If you're

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:26.760
not careful, AI will make your thinking

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.120
more average. So, I'll show you a

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.160
four-step framework to think so clearly

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.920
that people who you meet walk away

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.120
thinking you must be some kind of a

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.560
genius. Let's get started. For most of

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:43.230
my last 12 years, I slept for less than

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.520
four, five, six hours a night.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.680
>> [music]

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.840
>> I was the CEO of one of the top 20

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.120
fastest-growing companies in the US. I

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.520
worked at a large public company where

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.080
we were growing like crazy, and I

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.480
traveled nonstop, worked past midnight.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.480
So, every single morning when I woke up,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.160
I would always feel totally exhausted

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.120
from the first minute, and I would load

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.440
up on coffee, and [music] I would be

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:12.800
ready for the day. Then, um about 3

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:17.240
years ago, I retired from full-time

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.720
operator executive work, and my schedule

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.640
obviously changed dramatically. I had

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.480
more control over my day. I was on

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:26.920
boards, I was advising, I was investing,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.680
but it wasn't 7:00 a.m. till midnight

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:32.440
every single day. But still, every

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.320
morning I would wake up completely

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.840
exhausted.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.000
That was strange. So, I reached out to a

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:42.400
great pulmonologist and sleep

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.360
specialist, and they did a sleep study

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.560
on me, where they put me in a lab and

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:51.560
measured how I slept and what happened

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.760
in my brain. The medical data completely

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:58.880
blew my mind. The results showed that I

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.240
was waking up 34 times an hour, every

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.720
hour, all through the night. The

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:08.119
structure of my jaw is such that it was

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.640
blocking my airway. Now, I had no idea.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:13.200
I had spent years trying to normalize

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.280
the problem by telling myself, "Oh, I

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.320
need more sleep." Or, "I have to go

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.880
through this. It's the grind. I need

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:23.400
more coffee. I need to work less." The

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:26.320
moment I stopped perpetuating the story

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.760
and deconstructed the system from first

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.800
principles up, that fixed the root

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.080
cause. And I realized the problem was

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.280
not where I thought it was. That process

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.800
of thinking is called the first

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.240
principles thinking. We're going to

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.480
build a four-step framework here and

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:45.800
learn how to use AI to drive that

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:48.200
process and how to build specific

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:50.840
prompts for each of those steps. So,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:53.200
it's a bit dense, so I'll make a PDF as

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.400
well, so you can see all of it in one

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.440
place. Link is in the description. Of

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:01.720
course, it's free. We never investigate

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:04.519
our own assumptions. We inherit them

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.680
from our colleagues, our families,

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:09.480
industries, experts,

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.600
or our own previous experiences. First

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.239
principles want you to begin somewhere

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.920
else. What do I actually know for sure?

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:19.760
What am I assuming?

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.320
What else could produce this result?

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.640
Which part can I test? Imagine buying a

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.120
car,

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.320
driving it once, and then throwing it

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.760
away.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:32.320
That would sound insane, right? But for

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.440
decades, that was how most rockets

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.800
worked. Now, of course, rockets are not

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.760
cars. The physics is far more

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.040
unforgiving. Obviously, it's called

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.120
rocket science for a reason. But Elon

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:47.480
Musk questioned the very assumption. Why

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:50.160
couldn't the rocket land vertically

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.800
and fly again? That question changed the

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.320
economics of space travel. So, how do

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.480
start thinking in terms of first

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.560
principles?

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.480
Maybe a couple of examples. Maybe you

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:04.440
are thinking, "Mhm, I can't focus."

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:07.959
Well, start digging in. Is it focus or

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:10.400
sleep or phone addiction or unclear

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:12.680
tasks, too many priorities, you're

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.000
bored, there's no deadline, or are you

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.560
doing work with no real impact? What is

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.480
it? Things are about to get worse

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:23.680
because AI is here. Let's go there next.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.919
Consider how large language models

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.480
actually work. They do not physically

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:30.840
observe the universe we're in. They

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.520
don't have the idea of reality, at least

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.320
not yet. And they don't have the

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.760
real-world experience. They're

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:41.200
mathematical models that are very good

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:44.560
at matching patterns. So, when we ask a

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:47.040
question, it looks for the most likely

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.480
thing that matches the pattern. The most

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.280
familiar answer seems like the best

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:54.880
answer. That's the way AI is designed.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.880
And the better AI sounds, the less you

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.520
will check. A general-purpose AI model

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.520
does not naturally start from first

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.760
principles. That's why you have to force

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.760
AI to think differently. That's the

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.800
four-part framework. We'll walk through

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.520
each step, and we will also construct

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.840
prompts for each step. So, let's go to

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:17.480
the first step, which is D for

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.760
decompose. Let's say if you want to

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.960
start a business or even something

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.320
similar.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.040
Let's say you want to start a YouTube

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.440
channel. If you talk to the experts and

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.520
say, "Hey, I want to start a YouTube

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.160
channel. What do I need?" What are they

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.440
going to say? They'll say, "Oh, well,

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.760
you need a studio, you need a

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:37.680
professional camera, good lighting, uh

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.919
you need an editor, you need a creative

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.160
director, a production agency." And

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:45.520
sure, if you can afford all of that,

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:47.840
those things may save you some time and

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:51.360
improve the final product so you can

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:54.000
focus on content creation. But, that's

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.520
all conventional wisdom. What if you

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.200
boiled it down to its essential

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:01.560
components, decomposed? What do you

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:03.680
really need? Just two things: a phone

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.480
and a story. You can start tomorrow. You

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.320
can start tonight. The same idea applies

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.840
to starting a company. You can raise

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:14.840
millions from some investors. You can

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:17.560
rent a swanky office in New York City.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:20.840
But, there are thousands of successful

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:24.720
companies that got started without any

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:28.040
of that. So, this step of decomposing is

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:31.000
really about seeing the parts of the

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:32.800
machinery. This is where AI is going to

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.760
be tremendously useful. But, remember

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.120
that these models are also trained to be

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.360
very eager and very helpful. So, the

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.000
moment they see a problem, they would

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:44.600
want to solve it. You don't want that.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.880
You want them to break the problem down

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:50.080
into its essential parts. Let's apply

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:53.760
our friendly framework AIM to create a

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.520
prompt. I've covered the idea of AIM in

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.320
this video before somewhere. But, if you

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:02.640
haven't seen it, AIM stands for actor,

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:05.680
input, mission. Tell the model who it's

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:08.360
acting as. Give it the context or data

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.800
it needs. And then, tell it what done

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:13.880
looks like. So, here's the prompt. Act

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:17.360
as a world-class first principles

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:21.160
analyst. Your job in this step is

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:23.800
decomposition only. You're penalized for

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.480
introducing advice, solutions,

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:27.919
assumptions,

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:29.200
or standard playbooks. Be my thought

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:32.400
partner.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:35.040
Do not suck up to me. Intention.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.560
I want to understand exactly what this

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.880
problem is made up of.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:42.400
My problem is, and then you will insert

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:45.480
your problem. If the stated problem

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:46.720
appears to contain a hidden or a deeper

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.600
question,

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:52.320
identify it in one sentence before

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.760
decomposing the current problem. Ask

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.400
whether I want you to decompose the

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:59.320
original problem

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:03.280
or the deeper one. Do not continue until

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:05.600
I choose. Do not replace or reframe my

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.720
problem until I tell you. So, you see,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:10.440
you're trying to constrain what AI needs

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.440
to focus on. And the third part is

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:17.040
mission. Break the problem into its

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:19.720
smallest useful constituent parts. Show

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.440
the hierarchy clearly. The overall

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:25.520
problem, its major components, and the

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:29.040
smaller elements inside or under each

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:32.960
component. Use only dimensions that are

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:36.800
relevant, such as people, process steps,

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:39.560
time, resources, costs, etc. For each

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.039
component, briefly explain what it

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:45.320
contains and how it connects to the

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:47.280
larger problem. Stop decomposing when

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:49.040
going down further would no longer

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.920
improve my understanding. Do not

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:54.080
evaluate the components. Do not

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:56.240
recommend solutions. Only show me what

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.640
parts the problem is made up of. So,

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.480
that was your first prompt. And I'm

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.320
sorry if it was a bit long or

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:06.800
complicated, but I'm hoping that it will

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:10.800
give you clarity on how to boil down any

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.560
problem into its essential parts. That's

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.400
part of the first principles thinking.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.040
And also, how to make sure that AI

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.400
remains focused on the task that you

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.760
give it. Now, let's go to step two,

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:24.720
where we look at each of these parts and

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.760
see whether it's a fact or an

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.040
assumption. The most important first

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:32.680
principles skill is auditing the

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:35.280
assumptions. After the Second World War,

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:38.440
American car companies had enormous

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:42.200
factories that produced big American

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:45.120
cars for the big American market. Now,

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:47.520
Toyota was a very small company and they

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.120
were based in Japan and we're talking

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:53.000
about after World War II. So, Japan was

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:56.200
recovering, much less capital, fewer

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:58.800
resources, and a much smaller market.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:01.000
So, copying Detroit would not have

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.280
worked for Toyota. Toyota couldn't have

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:05.560
afforded it. So, they audited the

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:08.120
assumptions underneath the idea of mass

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:10.440
production itself. Why make big cars?

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.080
Why not small cars? Why produce them in

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:15.520
huge batches? Why do you need to hold

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.800
such a large inventory? What do you do

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.360
when there's a defect? Are there

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:23.360
different better ways to organize the

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:26.080
processes and people? That thinking

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.400
became the foundation of just-in-time

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:30.560
production and something that became so

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:33.160
dramatically successful that American

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:36.480
companies started implementing that and

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:38.800
over time Toyota overtook GM to become

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.880
the world's largest automaker. And, you

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.560
know, years later

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.360
I feel like history is repeating itself

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:49.400
because then came Tesla that questioned

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:52.280
the assumptions made by Toyota and all

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:54.680
car makers. Because Tesla asked, why do

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:57.920
you even need a combustion engine at

00:10:54.680 --> 00:11:00.600
all? And by 2020, Tesla had overtaken

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:03.360
Toyota as the world's most valuable

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:04.640
automaker. Most conventions that we

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:07.480
cling to

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:11.000
are just assumptions that have survived.

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:13.040
But, true innovation requires that you

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:16.320
break the rule, that you ignore the

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:18.839
conventions. And here's where AI can be

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:21.800
a very valuable partner. You can use the

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:24.080
model to perform that audit that we're

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:26.080
talking about. Here's the prompt to do

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:28.200
that and I'll just read the first part

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:30.640
of the prompt and then we'll just put

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:34.520
the entire prompt on the screen. You can

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:36.440
pause, and a screenshot or come back to

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:38.520
it anytime you want. And of course, all

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:41.200
of this is going to be in a nicely

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:44.360
formatted PDF. You can get it for free.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:48.800
All right. Here's the prompt. Actor.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:51.840
Act as a skeptical red team analyst

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:55.040
whose only job is to uncover and

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:59.080
question inherited assumptions. Assume

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:02.480
that every obvious part of the problem

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:05.840
may be hiding a convention until

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:08.000
evidence proves otherwise. Intention.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:11.240
I want to know which of the building

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:14.440
blocks above are assumptions.

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:15.800
And so on. So, this is the entire

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:16.880
prompt.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:20.200
Now,

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:23.360
once you identify the assumption,

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:25.280
you may feel like you

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:26.840
may have destroyed the entire solution

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.120
that you were thinking about. And that's

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:30.680
a good thing.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:33.080
Because now you finally have the freedom

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:35.240
to build a better solution from the

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:38.560
ground up. And for that, we go to the

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:41.560
next step, which is R for recombining. I

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:44.520
think music gives us the simplest way to

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:48.680
understand the idea of recombination.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:50.680
Most modern Western music is built from

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:53.200
12 notes. If you're trained in Western

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:55.120
music, that's your alphabet.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:58.360
You don't spend your entire life

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:00.280
searching for a secret 13th note. But if

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:02.920
you came from, let's say, Indian

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:05.400
classical music tradition, I came from

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:08.280
there, or Arabic music, they would use

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:11.839
pitches that fall between the 12 notes

